# spawn-guard-audience.declaration — WHOSE WORK EACH SPAWN GUARD'S REASON
#                                     PROTECTS, DECLARED AS DATA.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE DECISION THIS FILE IS
# ===========================================================================
# The CEO's §154 (2026-10-31): "RE-USE ALL OF THE FUCKING "WHEELS" 1:1 AND
# ONLY BUILD NEW "WHEELS" (OR MODIFY AS NEEDED) WHEN THERE'S NO EXISTING
# "WHEEL" OR SDK EQUIVALENT EXISTS FOR A GIVEN FEATURE/FUNCTION TO WORK IN THE
# APP." With §146 (every difference between the app and the terminal is his
# decision), this replaces Rich's ruling of 2026-09-18, which kept every guard
# whose reason was "the development session's" out of the app. The CEO ruled
# none of those off (Hank's audit, richos-hq docs/research/
# 2026-10-31-app-terminal-differences.md, finding 7); of his §146 answers only
# femcboost's own guard-brief-verification-scope.sh is off in the app, and it
# is not an engine guard, so it is not in hooks.json or here.
#
#     The RichOS app's prepare/spawn path is judged by the guards the
#     terminal's registration (hooks/hooks.json) runs on a dispatch, each
#     reading the user's own configuration (the app's coordination folder's
#     orchestration.config, built from the user's own) the way it reads the
#     repository's own in the terminal. A guard whose rule the user has not
#     declared stands down on its own, as it does in a terminal that never
#     adopted it. Only a guard that needs something the app does not have is
#     on the operator-session side, and its reason names that thing.
#
# So `user-work` now means "the app runs it" and `operator-session` means "it
# needs a terminal-only thing". The two words stay because every consumer and
# test already speaks them.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHY THIS IS ONE FILE OF DATA AND NOT A LIST TYPED IN TWO PLACES
# ===========================================================================
# It already WAS a list typed in two places, and that is the defect.
#
# The app's guard surface is pinned in two hand-written places: the plugin's
# `spawn-preflight.json`, written by `EngineProfile::prepare`
# (app/crates/richos-core/src/engine_profile.rs), which `spawn.py` reads
# through RICHOS_SPAWN_HOOK_SOURCES; and the Agent branch of
# `scripts/app-engine-hook.py`, which is the gate the platform actually runs
# at dispatch. Both were literal lists of two script paths. Nothing said WHY
# those two and nothing connected them to each other. That is not a bug
# anybody introduced; it is what an undeclared list becomes.
#
# So the classification lives HERE, once, in the shape
# `owned-systems.declaration` and `orchestration.config`'s model tiers already
# use: one declaration, quoted by consumers, never a second copy that drifts.
#
# Consumers:
#   scripts/lib/spawn-guard-audience.py   the parser and the classification
#   scripts/lib/spawn.py                  `--audience app` runs user-work only
#   scripts/app-engine-hook.py            the app's real PreToolUse[Agent] gate
#   app/crates/richos-core/src/engine_profile.rs
#                                         writes spawn-preflight.json from it
#
# Located by SPAWN_GUARD_AUDIENCE_DECLARATION in orchestration.config.
#
# ===========================================================================
# FORMAT — blank-line-separated records of `key: value`, one line per value
# ===========================================================================
#   id            the guard's file name, exactly as it is registered in
#                 hooks/hooks.json. This is the join; a rename here that is
#                 not a rename there is caught by the completeness test.
#   audience      `user-work` or `operator-session`. Nothing else is accepted.
#   reason        one sentence naming the guard's reason and why that reason
#                 puts it on this side: on the operator-session side, the
#                 terminal-only thing it needs.
#   user_message  REQUIRED on a `user-work` row, REFUSED on an
#                 operator-session row. What the app says to its user when
#                 THIS guard refuses their assignment — in the app's own
#                 words, addressed to a non-technical person, American
#                 English, naming no file, no flag and no acknowledgement
#                 line. It is the first line of the refusal; the guard's own
#                 message follows it unchanged, as the terminal's lead reads
#                 it, so the lead can fix the brief (§154). An operator-session
#                 row has none because the app never runs it.
#
# THE UNCLASSIFIED CASE, decided rather than left to a shrug: a guard
# registered in hooks.json and absent from this file is NOT run for the app.
# An undeclared guard has not been looked at for whether it can run in the
# app at all. The hazard that creates — a real user-work guard silently
# dropping off the app's list — is closed by the completeness test
# (scripts/lib/spawn-guard-audience.test.py), which fails the moment
# hooks.json and this file disagree in either direction.
#
# ===========================================================================
# USER-WORK — the app runs every one of these
# ===========================================================================

id: guard-sealed-worktree.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses every tool to a worker whose run has already ended, so a worker the platform restarted can never write into the user's project again; the thing on the other side of it is the user's own files.
user_message: The connection that asked for this work had already finished, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-worktree-isolation.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It requires every file-editing worker to get its own isolated copy of the repository before it starts, so two pieces of the user's work can never write over each other.
user_message: I could not give this work its own separate copy of your project, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-definition-drift.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses a worker whose instructions on disk no longer match the ones this session booted, so the user's work is never done against instructions that were replaced while it was being set up.
user_message: The instructions for this kind of work changed while I was setting it up, so I did not start it on the old ones. Nothing was created.

id: verify-agent-prompt.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It checks that the assignment handed to a worker is complete and its name is free — a duplicate worker, a missing role, or a worker being asked to hire other workers all produce work the user cannot use.
user_message: This assignment was not complete enough to hand to a worker, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-model-ceiling.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses a worker on a model tier above the declared cost ceiling, and the subscription that tier is charged to is the user's own.
user_message: This work asked for a more expensive model than this app is allowed to spend on, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-host-display-power.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses a command, brief, message or spawn that would change THIS machine's display, sleep, lock, session or input state, and the machine on the far side of a background assignment is the user's own, not a development session's.
user_message: This work asked to change your computer's screen, sleep, or lock settings, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-pierce.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It inspects the assignment and inherited instructions against the user's original request before a worker starts, returning findings to the planner for revision or explicit dismissal.
user_message: This assignment needs review, so I did not start the work. Nothing was created.

id: strip-ack-lines.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It never refuses anything; it removes operator acknowledgement lines from the assignment text before a worker sees it, so the user's own worker is never handed instructions written for the development session, and the app's spawn path applies it only when it is classified user-work.
user_message: This assignment could not be prepared for a worker, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-owned-state.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It never refuses; it tells the lead once per session when a standing system whose health the lead owns is known-bad, with the evidence and what makes it smaller, read from the owned-systems declaration the user's own configuration names (or the engine's).
user_message: I could not check the health of the systems this work depends on, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-no-home-network-phone.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses a dispatch that ties a phone surface to a home-network path unless the brief cites the ruling that allows it; the rule is on unless the user's own configuration switches it off (HOME_NETWORK_PHONE_GUARD), exactly as in the terminal.
user_message: This work would make a phone feature depend on your home network, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-brief-scope.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses a dispatch that drifts off a design-round specification recorded in the governed folder, and is silent where no such specification is recorded, which for most users is everywhere.
user_message: This work drifted from the design it was meant to follow, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-reference-ledger.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses a dispatch that rebuilds a surface the adoption ledger already answered unless the brief cites the row or argues why none applies (§66, the same rule as §154); it is on unless the user's own configuration switches it off (REFERENCE_LEDGER_GUARD).
user_message: This work would rebuild something that is already solved elsewhere without saying why, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

id: guard-public-record-repo.sh
audience: user-work
reason: It refuses work that would add a research read, brief or plan to a public folder instead of a private one, and stands down where the user declares no public/private split.
user_message: This work would put a private record into a public place, so I did not start it. Nothing was created.

# ===========================================================================
# OPERATOR-SESSION — each needs a terminal-only thing, so the app runs none of these
# ===========================================================================

id: guard-stale-staging.sh
audience: operator-session
reason: It needs the terminal's governed repository: it compares main and the deployment record of the one repository RICHOS_ENTITY_ROOT names, which in the app is the private coordination folder, not the user's product repository, so it would ask the wrong Git database and miss a stale staging or refuse current work (the second review of 2d5f7ded561a). Pointing it at a conversation's connected repositories, which can be several, is a separate change.

id: guard-live-names.sh
audience: operator-session
reason: It needs the terminal's operator mode: it refuses a spawn whose name a live agent of ANOTHER terminal orchestrator session on the same Mac already holds, and acts only while that mode is switched on, which no app lease ever does, so in the app it could only ever pass.

id: deliver-review-verdict.sh
audience: operator-session
reason: It never refuses anything and is not a dispatch check: registered for every tool, it hands a running teammate a second review's verdict. The app delivers that verdict through its own copy (scripts/lib/app_review.py mid_job_notice); moving the app onto this script is Hank's finding 9 (richos-hq docs/research/2026-10-31-app-terminal-differences.md), a separate change.

id: guard-operator-claim.sh
audience: operator-session
reason: It needs the terminal: it refuses the operator's own terminal session from acting on the team while the app holds a live claim on that team, and it passes every app-side spawn untouched by construction, so in the app it could only ever pass.
